rubric:
We changed one internal part of a small language model (0.6B parameters). Then we asked it three times to continue the prompt "The following is a short passage of text." Your job: say whether the change left a visible mark on the three texts, and if so, whether the mark is ONE thing or SEVERAL unrelated things.

Here are nine continuations from the UNCHANGED model. This is what normal output looks like: fluent but plain text, cut off after about 20 tokens, each on its own everyday topic. Normal texts often share openings ("The first...", "Today...", "In recent years...", a quote mark) and common words (people, world, government, study, problem). Small slips of a small model are normal too.
{refs}

Pick one label:
A = NO VISIBLE EFFECT. The three texts could have come from the unchanged model. An odd detail in only one of the three texts is not enough.
B = ONE THEME. One specific unusual thing shows up in at least two of the three texts: the same rare word, name, topic, foreign language or script, symbol, format, or one repeated token. The text may be broken or repetitive, as long as what comes back is that one thing.
C = SEVERAL THEMES. At least two of the texts are clearly abnormal, but in different, unrelated ways (different odd words, topics, languages or symbols), or broken with no single thing in common.

Three texts from the CHANGED model:
1: {t1}
2: {t2}
3: {t3}

Reply with JSON only: {{"thing": "<the one shared unusual thing in a few words, or empty>", "label": "A" or "B" or "C"}}
